# Shell scripts under poc/linux-client-test/ run INSIDE WSL2/Ubuntu (Linux) -
# force LF line endings regardless of the checkout platform or any local
# core.autocrlf setting, so a Windows checkout never silently reintroduces
# CRLF (which breaks `#!/usr/bin/env bash` shebangs and confuses bash).
poc/linux-client-test/*.sh eol=lf

# Same rationale, extended to vault-dns (WP 1.8): dnsmasq.conf.template is
# envsubst'd verbatim inside a Linux container (WP 1.9) -- a CRLF checkout
# would bake a trailing \r into every substituted line (e.g.
# address=.../CACHE_IP\r), which dnsmasq's parser does not tolerate and
# would fail to start on, not just a cosmetic issue. (The WSL2 helper
# dns/tests/functional-check.sh that shared this rule was retired by
# NGINX-DUP-1.)
dns/*.template eol=lf

# WP 1.9 (Docker packaging). Everything below is read by a Linux container
# runtime or shell, from a repository that is normally checked out on Windows.
# CRLF is not cosmetic here, it breaks things outright:
#   - .sh: a CRLF shebang line makes the kernel look for an interpreter named
#     "/bin/sh\r", which does not exist -> "no such file or directory" on a file
#     that visibly exists. Also silently appends \r to every string a script
#     compares or passes on.
#   - Dockerfile: a trailing \r ends up inside RUN commands and ENV values, so
#     e.g. VAULT_RESOLVER becomes "1.1.1.1\r" in the built image.
#   - nginx.conf.template: envsubst copies bytes verbatim, so \r would land in
#     the rendered nginx.conf; nginx treats it as part of the directive value.
core/docker/*.sh eol=lf
core/docker/*.template eol=lf
# NGINX-DUP-1: the hooks' default renders, compared byte for byte (cmp) with
# what the hooks write inside the Linux image.
core/docker/golden/*.conf eol=lf
dns/*.sh eol=lf
deploy/tests/*.sh eol=lf
**/Dockerfile eol=lf
deploy/compose.yaml eol=lf
# .dockerignore: patterns are matched verbatim, so a trailing \r makes a pattern
# match nothing (silently shipping what it was meant to exclude).
**/.dockerignore eol=lf
# .env.example is copied to .env and parsed by Compose; a trailing \r ends up
# inside the VALUE (e.g. an API key that never matches).
**/.env.example eol=lf

# WP 2.3 (vault-agent hosts mode) sandbox verification. Same shebang rationale
# as above: hosts-sandbox-container.sh is executed INSIDE an alpine container
# and run-hosts-sandbox.sh under WSL/bash, so a CRLF checkout makes the kernel
# look for an interpreter named "/bin/sh\r" and the script fails on a file that
# visibly exists. (The .ps1 in that directory runs on Windows and is left
# alone.)
agent/tests/sandbox/*.sh eol=lf

# WP EG-1 (ADR-0011, egress lock). vault-proxy's entrypoint and the hostname-
# validation helper it sources run INSIDE the container (same shebang
# rationale as every *.sh entry above); tinyproxy.conf is read verbatim by
# tinyproxy itself, so a trailing \r would land inside a directive value the
# same way it would in nginx.conf.template above. Found the hard way in
# round-2 review, not hypothetically: a CRLF checkout built a vault-proxy
# image whose entrypoint crash-looped with "no such file or directory"
# against a script that visibly existed, while vault-api kept reporting
# healthy (its own healthcheck is a loopback call, blind to the proxy
# container's state) and every outbound call failed. deploy/proxy/ did not
# exist when the entries above were written, so it was never covered.
deploy/proxy/*.sh eol=lf
deploy/proxy/*.conf eol=lf
